#03  A good law is a broken law
===============================================================================================

  For centuries, square-rooting a negative number was a big no-no.
  Let's say you were happily discriminating a polynomial on your spare time like a normal person would.
  And you ended up with negative numbers inside square roots.
  You had to stop and obey the commandment.

  Mathematicians started to think: "Come on, if ontological proof is ok, I'm not denying myself a workaround."
  (That's why you don't touch people's polynomials.)

  If we define:  i = √(-1)  then we can factor it out each time a square root receives a negative argument. Who's hurt?
  Turns out it allows a generalisation: any number in the set of complex numbers can take any number in the set of complex numbers
  as its exponent.  E.g.  (-6.66)^(1/16)  or  z^i
  Also turns out physicists and engineers ended up happy to have it ready to serve (Chapter I...).

  Lucky any question is allowed in mathematics and physics don't have to ask them first and wait for centuries.
  If mathematics were forced to have a point, they would have no point.
  Expecting maths to be immediately useful is asking physics to know the unknown.

  During a storm there's a voltage across the air between the sky and the earth. Sometimes lightning strikes.
  But before it happens we have U ≠ 0 and I = 0.
  So Ohm's law gives  R  =  U / 0.
  And it makes perfect sense: infinite Resistance is the extreme case that characterises an insulator.
  So  x / 0  =  sign (x) * ∞  makes logical sense and is the best way to describe a real-life phenomenon.

  I've heard someone say "only god is allowed to divide by zero".

  If god exists, let it strike me with lightning .R.I.G.H.T. .N.O.W.

